**Boss Station.** A decision tree recursively splits your feature space by asking yes/no questions. Each split is chosen to maximise information gain. The result is an interpretable model — a series of if/then rules anyone can read.

---

### Algorithm

```
function build_tree(X, y, depth, max_depth):
    if depth == max_depth or all(y) same class:
        return leaf(majority_class(y))

    best_feature, best_threshold = find_best_split(X, y)
    left  = build_tree(X[X[:,f] <= t], y[X[:,f] <= t], depth+1, max_depth)
    right = build_tree(X[X[:,f] >  t], y[X[:,f] >  t], depth+1, max_depth)
    return node(feature, threshold, left, right)
```

### Prediction

```
function predict(node, x):
    if leaf: return node.class
    if x[node.feature] <= node.threshold:
        return predict(node.left, x)
    else:
        return predict(node.right, x)
```

---

### Your Task

Implement `decision_tree_classify(X_train, y_train, X_test, max_depth=3)`:
- Build a tree to max_depth using Gini splits
- Find best split by trying all features and all unique midpoints as thresholds
- Return test accuracy (float, 4 dp)

